this is an explanation of the front and  back light animation matte process and  it was used the first time to my  knowledge to combine live action and  animation in saludos amigos  it was later used in 1947 to combine  stop-motion animation in a couple of  George pal Papa Tunes in this famous  example where Gene Kelly dances with  Jerry the mouse the question is always  raised how is it possible for Gene and  Jerry to have such a perfect interaction  almost as if they existed at the same  time in the same space well in the first  step of course Gene Kelly was  photographed against a live-action  background he's not matted into this  scene he's actually photographed into a  set that's art directed to look  something like an animation background  in the next step  jeans silhouette is traced frame by  frame onto paper this process is called  rotoscoping and in rotoscoping the  silhouette of Gene Kelly and any  additional background material are  copied onto animation paper a frame at a  time  registered precisely under the camera as  a color print of the live action is  projected onto it in this case about  10,000 frames of animation had to be  copied onto paper so the gene Kelly's  performance now existed in the animation  world in the next step the animation of  Jerry the mouse was created by the  traditional process beginning with a  pencil sketch again frame by frame of  the animation and this represents what's  called the cleanup sketch or the final  pencil stage of the animation hundreds  of tests each many thousands of frames  in length in some cases were required to  create the final action of the animation  that lined up and registered with Gene  Kelly that matched gene Kelly's eye line  and that matched his performance step  for step  unlike most animation which is  photographed on Tues that is two frames  per pose in this case it had to be  photographed on one's one frame per pose  to match gene Kelly's performance in the  next step when the animation was finally  approved the artist lays an animation  cell over the pencil sketch  and traces the animation figure in ink  onto a cell this ink sketch exists as a  separate plane on the transparent cell  in the next step the transparent cell is  colored from the back painted in eight  different colors in this case we're not  seeing one of the colors which are the  sometimes purple little pads on the  bottom of Jerry's feet and now the  animation frame by frame tens of  thousands of frames exist on tens of  thousands of transparent animation cells  the next question is how to composite  this image over the background now since  Jerry exists as a transparent cell we  can photograph him against any kind of  background if he were photographed  against a black background as you see  here and if that black background image  were double exposed on the optical  printer with the live-action photography  of Gene Kelly the result would be Jerry  the ghost  he's transparent in all the areas where  we don't want him to be and not a  convincing composite at all so the  question answered by this technique is  how do we keep Jerry's transparent image  from simply double exposing over the  background and the answer lies in the  nature of the opaque image painted on  the transparent cell let's say that  instead of photographing jury against  black we photographed him against a  white back light and let's say that  instead of lighting the cell from the  foreground let's say we turn that  lighting off the result is an opaque  silhouette and that opaque silhouette  can be photographed on film and that  opaque silhouette used in the optical  printer laid physically over the  background that we want to combine Jerry  with and it would look something like  this  here the silhouette matte as it's called  is by packed  or laid physically like a sandwich with  the background and the result is an  unexposed area exactly where we want  Jerry the mouse to be since it's  unexposed that means that we can in a  double exposure  take the front light exposure of Jerry  shot over black and double expose it  into the black area creating a perfect  fit like a piece of a puzzle  now these thousands of animation cells  photographed in color and in the black  and white silhouette and be combined  into a single  exactly as you see it in the dance  number and since Jerry and Jean both  existed together in the animation world  their reactions and actions can  correspond exactly there's only one  thing missing  you'll notice that jeans trousers and  shoes are casting a strong reflection in  the shiny background on which he's  standing and that reflection was  conspicuously missing from Jerry  probably in a second animation exposure  Jerry's reflection was double exposed to  single a simple double exposure no mat  necessary into the final shot and the  result is as you see it in the film
 

